#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# say

#% text
# `say(x1, x2, ...)` prints its arguments one after the other on a single line and gives `null`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Strings are printed as their text, without quotes; every other value is printed in its usual form, as `to_string` gives it.

#% item
# No separator is put between the arguments: write the spaces into the strings.

#% item
# The value is `null`, so a notebook cell or script that ends with `say` shows only the printed line.

#% item
# `say` is defined in the prelude as `print(string_join(map(to_string, parts)))`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
say("Hello, world")
#% output
#| Hello, world

#% text
# Text and values on one line:

#% cell
say("2^64 = ", 2^64)
#% output
#| 2^64 = 18446744073709551616

#% cell
x = 3/7
say("x = ", x, ", n(x) = ", n(x))
#% output
#| x = 3/7, n(x) = 0.42857142857142855

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Any value can be printed: lists and symbolic expressions:

#% cell
say("roots: ", solve(y^2 == 2, y))
#% output
#| roots: [[y -> -2^(1/2)], [y -> 2^(1/2)]]

#% cell
say("expression: ", expand((a + b)^3))
#% output
#| expression: 3*a*b^2 + 3*b*a^2 + a^3 + b^3

#% text
# Without arguments, an empty line:

#% cell
say()
#% output
#|

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Progress lines in a loop:

#% cell
for k in [1, 2, 3] { say("step ", k, " of 3: ", k^2) }
#% output
#| step 1 of 3: 1
#| step 2 of 3: 4
#| step 3 of 3: 9

#% text
# Reporting a numerical result with its check, as a script does:

#% cell
r = find_root(cos(t) - t, [t, 1])
say("root ", t /. r, ", residual ", (cos(t) - t) /. r)
#% output
#| root 0.7390851332151607, residual 0.0

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `print` takes a single value and gives it back:

#% cell
print("a")
#% output
#| a
#= "a"

#% cell
say("a", 1, "b")
#% output
#| a1b

#% text
# The value of `say` is `null`:

#% cell
say("value") === null
#% output
#| value
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Numbers are not separated automatically:

#% cell
say(1, 2, 3)
#% output
#| 123

#% text
# A failure among the arguments is not printed: it is the value of the call:

#% cell
say("result: ", failure(not_found, "key"))
#% output
#! failure(not_found, "key")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `print`, `to_string`, `string_join`, `timing`
